.SH DESCRIPTION
asn1c compiles ASN.1 specifications into C/C++ encoders and decoders for
BER, DER, CER, PER, XER, OER, JER, CBOR and other encoding rules.
Its optional \f[B]\-\-rust\f[R] mode generates a Rust Cargo project for
the supported subset.
.PP
The \f[B]\-\-rust\f[R] mode generates a standalone Cargo project with a
safe runtime using the Rust 2021 edition.
The current subset provides UPER encoding and decoding, and strict CER
encoding and decoding, for top\-level BOOLEAN, NULL, and finite
non\-extensible INTEGER ranges fitting \f[CR]i64\f[R].
Opt\-in BER/DER, APER, OER, and JER cover those primitives.
UPER alone also supports fixed strings, ENUMERATED, and flat SEQUENCE
with required or OPTIONAL direct fields.
Other types and codec combinations produce a diagnostic until
implemented.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Stage Selection Options
.TP
\-\-rust
Select the complete Rust output mode, including Rust types, codec
methods, Cargo metadata, and a copied native Rust runtime.
The current default codec set is UPER and CER encoding.
\f[CR]\-no\-gen\-UPER\f[R] selects CER\-only output;
\f[CR]\-no\-gen\-CER\f[R] leaves UPER enabled.
Unavailable codec requests fail; \f[CR]\-ftolerant\-utc\-time\f[R] is
supported and selects legacy UTCTime BER decoding.
The default output directory in this mode is \f[CR]asn1c\-rust\f[R].
Build the generated package with
\f[CR]cargo check \-\-offline \-\-manifest\-path asn1c\-rust/Cargo.toml\f[R].
.PP
For example, save this schema as \f[CR]Demo.asn1\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
Demo \f[B]DEFINITIONS\f[R] ::= \f[B]BEGIN\f[R]
Flag ::= BOOLEAN
\f[B]END\f[R]
.EE
.PP
Generate and check it with:
.IP
.EX
asn1c \-\-rust \-D generated Demo.asn1
cargo check \-\-offline \-\-manifest\-path generated/Cargo.toml
.EE
.TP
\-E
Run the ASN.1 parsing stage only.
Print the reconstructed ASN.1 tree (text).
.TP
\-F
Used together with \f[B]\-E\f[R], instructs the compiler to stop after
the ASN.1 syntax tree fixing stage and dump the reconstructed ASN.1
specification to the standard output.
.TP
\-P
Dump the compiled output to the standard output instead of creating the
target language files on disk.
.TP
\-R
Restrict the compiler to generate only the ASN.1 tables, omitting the
usual support code.
.TP
\-S \f[I]directory\f[R]
Use the specified directory with ASN.1 skeleton files.
.TP
\-D \f[I]directory\f[R]
Destination directory for generated files (default current directory for
C, or \f[CR]asn1c\-rust\f[R] for Rust).
Rust mode stages output and rejects unrelated existing files or symlinks
in the destination.
.TP
\-X
Generate an XML DTD schema for the specified ASN.1 files.
.SS Warning Options
.TP
\-Werror
Treat warnings as errors; abort if any warning is produced.
.TP
\-Wdebug\-lexer
Enable lexer debugging during the ASN.1 lexing stage.
.TP
\-Wdebug\-parser
Enable lexer debugging during the ASN.1 parsing stage.
.TP
\-Wdebug\-fixer
Enable ASN.1 syntax tree fixer debugging during the fixing stage.
.TP
\-Wdebug\-compiler
Enable debugging during the actual compile time.
.SS Language Options
.TP
\-fbless\-SIZE
Allow \f[CR]SIZE()\f[R] constraint for \f[CR]INTEGER\f[R],
\f[CR]ENUMERATED\f[R], and other types for which this constraint is
normally prohibited by the standard.
This is a violation of ASN.1 standard, and the compiler may fail to
produce a meaningful code.
.TP
\-fcompound\-names
Using this option prevents name collisions in the target source code by
using complex names for target language structures.
(Name collisions may occur if the ASN.1 module reuses the same
identifiers in multiple contexts).
.TP
\-findirect\-choice
When generating code for a \f[CR]CHOICE\f[R] type, compile the
\f[CR]CHOICE\f[R] members as indirect pointers instead of declaring them
inline.
Consider using this option together with \f[B]\-fno\-include\-deps\f[R]
to prevent circular references.
.TP
\-fincludes\-quoted
Refer to header files in \f[CR]#include\f[R]s using
\f[B]\(dq\f[R]double\f[B]\(dq\f[R] instead of
\f[B]<\f[R]angle\f[B]>\f[R] quotes.
.TP
\-fknown\-extern\-type=\f[I]name\f[R]
Pretend the specified type is known.
The compiler will assume the target language source files for the given
type have been provided manually.
.TP
\-fline\-refs
Include ASN.1 module\(aqs line numbers in generated code comments.
.TP
\-fno\-constraints
Do not generate ASN.1 subtype constraint checking code.
This may make a shorter executable.
.TP
\-ftolerant\-utc\-time
Keep legacy permissive \f[CR]UTCTime\f[R] decoding.
By default, the C runtime and generated Rust BER decoder validate
UTCTime syntax, clock ranges, and calendar dates independently of
generated subtype constraint checks; C\(aqs
\f[CR]\-fno\-constraints\f[R] does not turn off this validation.
Strict mode accepts optional seconds and requires \f[CR]Z\f[R] or a
complete \f[CR]+hhmm\f[R] / \f[CR]\-hhmm\f[R] offset.
The legacy year mapping remains \f[CR]00..59\f[R] to 2000..2059 and
\f[CR]60..99\f[R] to 1960..1999; it is not an RFC 5280 profile check.
For C output, this option emits \f[CR]ASN_ENABLE_TOLERANT_UTCTIME\f[R]
in generated \f[CR]asn_config.h\f[R].
When using a separately built C runtime, define the macro while
compiling the runtime skeleton objects; defining it only in application
code does not affect a prebuilt runtime.
With \f[CR]\-R\f[R], build the runtime with the macro directly because
runtime support and its configuration header are omitted.
For Rust output, the generated type selects a tolerant BER decoder
without a Cargo feature; DER encoding remains raw and CER validation
remains strict.
The strict UTCTime constraint callback checks syntax and calendar fields
without converting through \f[CR]time_t\f[R].
\f[CR]asn_UT2time()\f[R] does convert through \f[CR]time_t\f[R], returns
\f[CR](time_t)\-1\f[R] for errors, and may return the same value for the
valid UTC instant 1969\-12\-31 23:59:59; its range also depends on the
platform\(aqs \f[CR]time_t\f[R] representation.
See X.680 47.2 and 47.3 at \c
.UR https://www.itu.int/rec/T-REC-X.680-202102-I/en
.UE \c
\&.
The option affects UTCTime only; GeneralizedTime behavior is unchanged.
.TP
\-fno\-include\-deps
Do not generate courtesy #include lines for non\-critical type
dependencies.
Helps prevent namespace collisions.
.TP
\-fprefer\-import\-source
Resolve only names that an IMPORTS group lists (\f[CR]xp_members\f[R]).
In both modes, a name listed in any IMPORTS group resolves to the module
that its group names (X.680 13.19).
Without this option, a name that no IMPORTS group lists is also searched
for in the body of each FROM module (whole\-module fallback), which
X.680 does not allow.
Enable this option to disable that fallback.
.TP
\-fprefix=\f[I]prefix\f[R]
Add the specified prefix to all generated type names and filenames.
This helps avoid naming conflicts in several scenarios:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]System header conflicts\f[R]: On case\-insensitive filesystems
(macOS HFS+, Windows), ASN.1 types like \(dqTime\(dq would generate
\f[CR]Time.h\f[R], which can conflict with system header
\f[CR]<time.h>\f[R].
asn1c now automatically disambiguates these generated filenames (for
example, \f[CR]Time.h\f[R] becomes \f[CR]asn1c_time.h\f[R] when no
explicit prefix is set).
Likewise, types whose names match a runtime skeleton file
case\-insensitively (e.g.
\(dqNull\(dq vs \f[CR]NULL.h\f[R], \(dqInteger\(dq vs
\f[CR]INTEGER.h\f[R]) are generated as \f[CR]asn1c_Null.h\f[R],
\f[CR]asn1c_Integer.h\f[R], etc.
Using \f[CR]\-fprefix=ASN1_\f[R] still generates \f[CR]ASN1_Time.h\f[R]
when you need a project\-specific naming convention.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Multiple ASN.1 modules\f[R]: When generating code for multiple
ASN.1 syntaxes that have type name clashes, a prefix prevents symbol
collisions.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Integration with existing code\f[R]: Prefixes help avoid conflicts
with existing types in your codebase.
.PP
\f[B]Important\f[R]: use this flag when you want a consistent custom
namespace for all generated symbols and filenames, especially when
integrating multiple schemas.
.PP
Example: \f[CR]asn1c \-fprefix=PKIX_ rfc3280.asn1\f[R]
.TP
\-funnamed\-unions
Enable unnamed unions in the definitions of target language\(aqs
structures.
.TP
\-fwide\-types
Use the unbounded size data types (\f[CR]INTEGER_t\f[R],
\f[CR]ENUMERATED_t\f[R], \f[CR]REAL_t\f[R]) by default, instead of using
the native machine\(aqs data types (long, double).
.TP
\-flong\-size=\f[I]bits\f[R]
Select the target C \f[CR]long\f[R] model used by the default native
INTEGER storage policy.
\f[I]bits\f[R] is one of \f[CR]32\f[R] or \f[CR]64\f[R]; the default
\f[CR]auto\f[R] behavior preserves the historical portable 32\-bit
assumption.
Use this option when generating code on one platform for a target with a
different \f[CR]long\f[R] size.
.TP
\-finteger\-native\-type=\f[I]mode\f[R]
Select the fixed\-width native C storage policy for constrained ASN.1
INTEGER types.
\f[I]mode\f[R] is one of \f[CR]auto\f[R], \f[CR]int32\f[R],
\f[CR]uint32\f[R], \f[CR]int64\f[R], or \f[CR]uint64\f[R]; the default
is \f[CR]auto\f[R].
\f[CR]auto\f[R] preserves the traditional storage decision:
\f[CR]long\f[R] or \f[CR]unsigned long\f[R] for ranges that fit the
target \f[CR]long\f[R] model, and \f[CR]INTEGER_t\f[R] otherwise.
\f[CR]int32\f[R]/\f[CR]int64\f[R] permit signed fixed\-width storage for
ranges that fit signed 32\-bit or 64\-bit values; \f[CR]uint32\f[R]/
\f[CR]uint64\f[R] permit unsigned fixed\-width storage only when the
compiler can prove a non\-negative bounded range that fits unsigned
32\-bit or 64\-bit values.
Values that do not fit the selected native policy are generated as
\f[CR]INTEGER_t\f[R].
Unsigned upper bounds such as \f[CR]18446744073709551615\f[R] are
preserved without signed wraparound, and bounds beyond 64 bits are
stored as static arbitrary\-precision constraint values.
