.PP
CER encoding uses \f[CR]cer_encode()\f[R] or
\f[CR]asn_encode(ATS_CER, ...)\f[R]; the generated converter accepts
\f[CR]\-ocer\f[R] output.
The C backend generates CER support by default, and the
\f[CR]ASN_DISABLE_CER_SUPPORT\f[R] build macro disables both the encoder
and the strict decoder.
The generated converter\(aqs \f[CR]\-iber\f[R] and \f[CR]\-ider\f[R]
selectors use the permissive BER decoder.
\f[CR]cer_decode()\f[R], \f[CR]asn_decode(ATS_CER, ...)\f[R], and the
\f[CR]\-icer\f[R] selector decode with the BER decoder, re\-encode the
value with \f[CR]cer_encode()\f[R], and fail with \f[CR]EBADF\f[R]
unless the re\-encoding is byte\-identical to the consumed input.
Non\-canonical input such as long\-form lengths, definite\-length
constructed values, non\-minimal INTEGER, unsorted SET OF, or
unsegmented long strings is rejected.
Trailing bytes after the first TLV are not consumed.
\f[CR]\-icer\f[R] requires CER generation and buffers the whole input
rather than decoding incrementally.
Type support depends on the generated descriptor and canonical value
forms supported by the encoder.
Opaque ANY and unresolved standalone open types are rejected.
Raw REAL accepts zero, special values, and minimal base\-2 forms with
exponents of at most eight octets; decimal or non\-canonical raw forms
are rejected.
UTCTime is normalized to UTC seconds.
GeneralizedTime requires an explicit zone and supports at most nine
fractional\-second digits; comma input is normalized to a period.
Constructed character strings retain their outer type tag and use
universal OCTET STRING tag 4 for fragments.
BIT STRING fragments use universal tag 3.
BER input preserves legacy opaque ANY containers while validating their
lengths, nesting, and EOC markers; typed string wrappers require one
child.
The encoder applies finite output, scratch, collection, work, recursion,
and descriptor tag\-chain limits.
An additional explicit member tag counts against the tag\-chain cap; see
the binary codec support documentation for defaults.
.PP
The direct \f[CR]cer_encode_to_buffer()\f[R] function fails if the
caller\(aqs buffer is too small.
The generic \f[CR]asn_encode_to_buffer(ATS_CER, ...)\f[R] follows the
generic buffer API: \f[CR].encoded\f[R] reports the full required size,
and a short buffer contains only an output prefix.
.PP
The generated converter can read BER and write CER with
\f[CR]./converter\-example \-iber \-ocer input.ber > output.cer\f[R].
.PP
The Rust backend currently encodes CER for BOOLEAN, NULL, and bounded
non\-extensible INTEGER ranges fitting \f[CR]i64\f[R], and generates a
strict \f[CR]decode_cer()\f[R] that accepts only the canonical
short\-form TLV.
Its UPER and CER codecs are enabled by default.
To generate only the CER methods, pass
\f[CR]\-\-rust \-no\-gen\-UPER\f[R].
.PP
*) Asterisk means both BASIC and CANONICAL variants.
Pass \f[CR]XER_F_BASIC\f[R] or \f[CR]XER_F_CANONICAL\f[R] to
\f[CR]xer_encode()\f[R] for the corresponding XER variant.
Use \f[CR]JER_F_MINIFIED\f[R] with \f[CR]jer_encode()\f[R] for
whitespace\-free output; \f[CR]jer_decode()\f[R] accepts either JER
form.
.SH XER AND JER ENCODING INSTRUCTIONS
asn1c supports a selected set of schema\-level encoding instructions for
XER and JER.
Instructions may be written as bracketed type prefixes or in
\f[CR]ENCODING\-CONTROL\f[R] sections.
.IP
.EX
Flag ::= [TEXT] BOOLEAN
Mode ::= [XER:TEXT] ENUMERATED { idle(0), busy(1) }
Blob ::= [JER:BASE64] OCTET STRING

ENCODING\-CONTROL XER
    GLOBAL\-DEFAULTS MODIFIED\-ENCODINGS
    DECIMAL Ratio
    TEXT Count.one AS \(dquno\(dq
\f[B]END\f[R]

ENCODING\-CONTROL JER
    BASE64 Blob
    TEXT Mode.busy AS \(dqoccupied\(dq
    NAME Packet.payload AS \(dqpayload64\(dq
\f[B]END\f[R]
.EE
.PP
Supported XER instructions:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]BASE64\f[R] for \f[CR]OCTET STRING\f[R]; bare \f[CR][BASE64]\f[R]
remains XER for compatibility.
.IP \(bu 2
legacy \f[CR]Type OCTET STRING ::= hexadecimal\f[R], \f[CR]base64\f[R],
and \f[CR]utf8\f[R] forms.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]TEXT\f[R] for \f[CR]BOOLEAN\f[R], \f[CR]ENUMERATED\f[R],
named\-number \f[CR]INTEGER\f[R], and named\-bit \f[CR]BIT STRING\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]DECIMAL\f[R] for \f[CR]REAL\f[R], only when
\f[CR]GLOBAL\-DEFAULTS MODIFIED\-ENCODINGS\f[R] is present in the XER
control section.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]GLOBAL\-DEFAULTS MODIFIED\-ENCODINGS\f[R].
.PP
Supported JER instructions:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]BASE64\f[R] for \f[CR]OCTET STRING\f[R]; use
\f[CR][JER:BASE64]\f[R] for type prefixes.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]TEXT Type.value AS \(dqjson\-string\(dq\f[R] for named values of
\f[CR]ENUMERATED\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]NAME Type.member AS \(dqjson\-key\(dq\f[R] for members of
\f[CR]SEQUENCE\f[R], \f[CR]SET\f[R], and \f[CR]CHOICE\f[R].
.PP
The compiler rejects incompatible targets, unknown targets, missing
\f[CR]AS\f[R] values where required, XER \f[CR]DECIMAL\f[R] without
\f[CR]GLOBAL\-DEFAULTS MODIFIED\-ENCODINGS\f[R], and duplicate JER wire
names within the same constructed type.
JER \f[CR]NAME\f[R] affects JSON keys only; it does not rename generated
C fields or XER XML tags.
.SH CBOR TAGS
CBOR (RFC 8949) supports \f[I]tags\f[R] (major type 6) as optional
semantic annotations on any data item.
A tag consists of a tag number followed by the tagged value.
Common tag numbers are registered by IANA at: \c
.UR https://www.iana.org/assignments/cbor-tags/
.UE \c
.PP
\f[B]Encoding:\f[R] Call
\f[CR]cbor_encode_tag(tag_number, cb, app_key)\f[R] immediately before
encoding the value to prepend a tag header.
Symbolic constants for well\-known tags (e.g.,
\f[CR]CBOR_TAG_DATETIME_STRING\f[R], \f[CR]CBOR_TAG_URI\f[R],
\f[CR]CBOR_TAG_SELF_DESCRIBED\f[R]) are defined in
\f[CR]cbor_support.h\f[R].
.PP
\f[B]Decoding:\f[R] All asn1c CBOR decoders are
\f[I]tag\-transparent\f[R]: any number of leading tag headers are
silently consumed before the underlying value is decoded.
No application changes are required to accept tagged data.
The helper \f[CR]cbor_skip_tags(buf, size)\f[R] (in
\f[CR]cbor_support.h\f[R]) returns the number of bytes occupied by
leading tag headers, or \-1 on error.
When decoder code must skip a complete raw CBOR value, it should call
\f[CR]cbor_skip_item_with_ctx(opt_codec_ctx, buf, size)\f[R] so
recursive arrays, maps, and tags are bounded by the decoder stack limit.
The compatibility wrapper \f[CR]cbor_skip_item(buf, size)\f[R] remains
available for callers without decoder context.
.PP
\f[B]Bignum tags:\f[R] Tags 2 and 3 are used internally by the INTEGER
encoder and decoder for values that exceed the 64\-bit signed range, per
RFC 8949.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
\f[B]0\f[R]
Success.
No FATAL diagnostic was reported.
.TP
\f[B]64\f[R] (\f[CR]EX_USAGE\f[R])
Command line usage error, or no input files.
.TP
\f[B]65\f[R] (\f[CR]EX_DATAERR\f[R])
ASN.1 input error: a syntax error, or a FATAL diagnostic during semantic
processing (\f[B]\-F\f[R] and code generation).
.TP
\f[B]66\f[R] (\f[CR]EX_NOINPUT\f[R])
An input file cannot be opened.
.TP
\f[B]70\f[R] (\f[CR]EX_SOFTWARE\f[R])
Printing or code generation failed, or reported a FATAL diagnostic.
Files can be written, but the output is incomplete.
.TP
\f[B]72\f[R] (\f[CR]EX_OSFILE\f[R])
Skeleton files were not found and \f[B]\-Werror\f[R] is in effect.
.PP
A FATAL diagnostic always gives a non\-zero exit status.
